# Practical 2 — Multilayer Perceptron (MLP) for Classification

## Aim

To design and implement a Multilayer Perceptron (MLP) for classification of the Iris dataset and evaluate its performance using accuracy and a confusion matrix.

## Dataset

Iris Dataset

## Tools and Libraries Used

- Python
- Google Colab
- TensorFlow
- Keras
- NumPy
- Scikit-learn
- Matplotlib

## Dataset Description

The Iris dataset contains 150 flower samples belonging to 3 different classes:

- Setosa
- Versicolor
- Virginica

Each sample contains 4 features:

- Sepal Length
- Sepal Width
- Petal Length
- Petal Width

## Steps Performed

1. Loaded the Iris dataset using Scikit-learn.
2. Separated the input features and target labels.
3. Split the dataset into training and testing sets using an 80:20 ratio.
4. Standardized the input features using StandardScaler.
5. Designed an MLP using TensorFlow/Keras.
6. Used two hidden layers with 16 and 8 neurons.
7. Used ReLU activation in the hidden layers.
8. Used Softmax activation in the output layer.
9. Compiled the model using the Adam optimizer and sparse categorical crossentropy loss.
10. Trained the model for 50 epochs.
11. Evaluated the model using test accuracy.
12. Generated predictions for the test data.
13. Created a confusion matrix to analyze the classification results.

